{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the flood was.</b> One may ask: what difference was there that the Holy One, blessed be He, brought a flood more than the other plagues? Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: they sinned with boiling waters and with boiling waters they were punished. But one may compare the matter to a king who built a fine dwelling for a group of blind men and ordered that they be provided for generously. And whenever the king would pass through there, they would stand and bow toward the king and praise him in a loud voice. Once the king heard their voices. He said, 'What is this sound in my ears?' They told him, 'They are the blind men whom you ordered to be provided for there, and so they do every time they hear the sound of your horses.' He said, 'If they could see me, how much more — and how much more — would they praise me!' Immediately he ordered to send them back and to put in their place sighted people, and to provide for them as he had for the blind. But those people, once they saw themselves in comfort, grew arrogant, and when they saw the king passing by they would curse him and say, 'Woe to this one who has subjugated us.' Another time the king heard their voices and asked what they were saying. They told him, 'They are cursing you, and they do not behave as the blind men whom you sent back did.' The king ordered that they be killed. So too the world at first was entirely water, which neither sees nor hears, yet even so they praised the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said: נשאו נהרות ה׳ etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'If I were to create man, who hears and sees, how much praise and how much laudation would they give me!' Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded: יקוו המים — and ותראה היבשה, and in it He created man in place of the waters, and bestowed goodness upon them, so that they would sow the land and it would yield produce for them for forty years. And from the abundance of goodness they rebelled against the Holy One, blessed be He. The Holy One, blessed be He, heard their rebellion and commanded the waters to come upon them and wash them away. Rabbi Shemariah said: he heard this from the mouth of Rabbi Natan Ofniel."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The place name 'אפניאל' is uncertain; rendered as 'Ofniel' on the assumption of a toponymic or family name."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:13.077193557Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}